fix(scripts): write a schedule unit on a host with no scheduler installed

OPS-035 installs a schedule and reads the unit back. It passed here and failed
in CI, because `platform` answered two questions at once and only one of them
matters when writing a file.

A Linux container usually has no `systemctl`. `platform` returned empty, install
refused as unsupported, and the test failed having proven nothing about the unit
it exists to check -- the failure printed the unit's own text, which is what a
fatal install does.

Writing a unit needs the OS layout. Loading one needs the scheduler. Those are
separate, so they are separate functions now, and `--no-load` may fall back to
the layout: nothing is handed to a scheduler, so the absence of one is not a
reason to refuse. Without the flag a host with no scheduler still cannot
install, which is the honest answer there.

Reproduced the CI condition by running install with systemctl off the PATH:
refused before, writes the unit after. The refusal path is unchanged and still
asserted.

